  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 315 PM Saturday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Another Heat Advisory has been issued for parts of \nthe Triangle and south central NC for Sun. The hot weather will \ncontinue into mid week, peaking on Monday when most of the area will \nhave heat index values near or over 105F, and our southeast could \nsee 110F or higher. \n\nStrong heating across southern portions of central NC along with \nhigh dewpoints have pushed heat index values to around 105F today, \nwhile over NE and far N sections, this morning's sct-bkn stratus \nfractus curbed heating a bit, keeping heat indices in the 90s to \naround 100F. These clouds were along and NE of a surface boundary \nevident in recent analyses stretching over NW into E NC, with \nslightly more stable air noted earlier today NE of this boundary. \nThis boundary should wash out and dissipate late tonight, putting us \nback into a light flow from the SSE to SW Sun and pushing high temps \nwell into the 90s most areas. Despite some modest mixing of surface \ndewpoints Sun afternoon, readings should still hold in the low-mid \n70s, with some upper 70s persisting over parts of the Coastal Plain, \nand this will result in heat index values peaking around 105 to 109 \nover much of our southern half and Triangle region, where heat \nisland effects could further boost local temps. We've issued another \nHeat Advisory for Sun for this area. But there is some uncertainty \nwith this, as our confidence is increasing that we'll have areas of \nlow stratus around the area Sun morning, not dissipating \nsubstantially until late morning or the noon hour, which could put a \ndamper on strong heating in some areas. If this occurs, heat index \nvalues could be a bit lower than expected, although this could be \nbalanced by less mixing of surface dewpoints. Otherwise, thicknesses \nare projected to be around 10 m above normal, so highs well into the \n90s remain plausible. \n\nThis hot stretch is likely to be its worst on Mon, when highs are \nexpected to reach the mid 90s to around 100F areawide, with elevated \ndewpoints persisting, pushing the heat index over 105F over all but \nthe far N, and perhaps over 110F over our far SE. If confidence in \nthis continues to grow, additional Heat Advisories will be needed, \nand an Extreme Heat Watch or Warning may be considered in some \nareas for Mon. \n\nTemps will remain above normal with high humidity through mid week, \nbut with slightly lower intensity. The mid level ridge to our S will \ngradually retrograde in response to a southward progression of the \nflat NW flow from the Midwest through VA/NC, nudging thicknesses \ndown a bit, although our SW areas may stay atypically hot into late \nweek.\n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A Marginal (level 1 of 5) risk of severe \nthunderstorms continues through this evening over all of central NC, \nand over the north Sunday. Damaging winds are the primary threat. \n\nFor another day, we're in a regime featuring high PW aoa 2\" and mdt-\nhigh SBCAPE of 1500-3000 J/kg, highest in the S and far W, but with \nmodest mid level lapse rates and poor deep layer bulk shear, \nparticularly in the area of highest CAPE, resulting in low overlap \nof the most favorable CAPE/shear. Predictability of precise location \nand timing of convection remains somewhat low beyond the first 24 \nhours, as the primary features that are focusing convection remain \nmultiple weak outflows from upshear convective systems, along with \nthe sea breeze and terrain-favored storms rolling into the W \nPiedmont. The sub-20-kt deep layer shear will continue to limit \nstorm organization and longevity, but a few strong storms producing \nwater-loaded downdrafts remain possible through mid evening. This \noverall regime will hold for Sun as well, primarily (but not solely) \nacross the north as outflows drift in from the NW and N, and where \nPWs are expected to be a bit higher as compared to our SW sections.",
